// The words of krypton's "What was hiding in the air?" room, in English, European Portuguese, Polish and
// German, kid (Explorer) and sci (Scientist). The picture's own story stays in src/content/boards/Kr.js (the
// easel shows it); the room shows it happening and adds to it. Signs drawn in 3D hold no superscripts ("cm3");
// the panels (HTML) write cm³.
// Facts and where they come from: Krypton 1.14 ppm of dry air by volume; boiling points N2 -195.8, Ar -185.9,
// O2 -183.0, Kr -153.2, Xe -108.1 degrees C (src/data/elements.json). Hampson's liquefier (patented 1895)
// supplied about 750 cm3 of liquid air in May 1898, Travers kept the gas of the last 10 cm3 (Dictionary of
// Scientific Biography, Travers; Travers, The Discovery of the Rare Gases, 1928). Red-hot copper for oxygen
// and red-hot magnesium for nitrogen are the methods Ramsay's laboratory used on air (argon, 1894). "About 26
// cm3" of residue is the figure usually given (DSB). Krypton's yellow (587 nm) and green (557 nm) lines. The
// discovery day (30 or 31 May 1898) is said only as "the end of May". Neon followed in June and xenon in July
// 1898; Ramsay's Nobel Prize in Chemistry and Rayleigh's in Physics, 1904. Welsbach's gas mantle, 1880s. The
// "last 10 cm3 holds about 70 krypton per million molecules" is OUR estimate (750 cm3 of liquid air is about
// 22 mol, 1.14 ppm of it is about 26 micromol; 10 cm3 of mostly liquid oxygen is about 0.36 mol; if nearly all
// the krypton stayed there that is about 70 per million), and the room says it is drawn from an estimate.
